WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.260
A physician castrated young roosters.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.980
Then he put a testis back into the belly of some of them, cut off from its original

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.640
nerves. Those birds still grew combs and behaved like roosters.

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.320
How can an organ change the whole body without its own nerves?

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.140
In this bird, the testis acted through something carried in the blood.

6
00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:34.603
A hormone is a chemical that one group of cells releases into the blood.

7
00:00:35.303 --> 00:00:38.003
The blood carries it to distant target cells.

8
00:00:38.923 --> 00:00:40.863
Those cells have receptors for it.

9
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:46.283
A receptor is a protein that binds the hormone and changes what the cell does.

10
00:00:47.323 --> 00:00:48.623
Think of a radio broadcast.

11
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:53.683
It reaches every house, and only houses with a tuned set hear it.

12
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.507
In mammals, the hypothalamus sits at the base of the brain.

13
00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.787
It controls the pituitary gland.

14
00:01:01.687 --> 00:01:03.507
Take the stress axis in humans.

15
00:01:04.327 --> 00:01:11.567
The hypothalamus releases CRH, and CRH makes the anterior pituitary release ACTH.

16
00:01:12.247 --> 00:01:15.887
Then ACTH makes the adrenal cortex produce cortisol.

17
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.690
Now the loop closes.

18
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:24.590
In humans, rising cortisol lowers CRH and ACTH.

19
00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:29.910
That is negative feedback, a response that opposes the change that caused it.

20
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:34.630
So a rise in cortisol reduces the drive that produced it.

21
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.750
Feedback acts at both the hypothalamus and the pituitary.

22
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:43.733
Feedback does not hold a hormone at one fixed value.

23
00:01:44.493 --> 00:01:47.973
In humans, cortisol rises and falls over the day.

24
00:01:48.593 --> 00:01:52.613
It also comes in pulses roughly every 60 to 90 minutes.

25
00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:59.593
In a study of 15 healthy men, whose own cortisol was blocked, the same daily dose on

26
00:01:59.593 --> 00:02:03.933
different schedules changed how their brains responded to emotional stimuli.

27
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:06.153
Timing carries information.

28
00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:12.597
Other animals run the same logic on different molecules, so name the animal each

29
00:02:12.597 --> 00:02:19.677
time. In the bug Rhodnius prolixus, a signal from the head travels in the blood and

30
00:02:19.677 --> 00:02:20.797
is needed for the moult.

31
00:02:21.897 --> 00:02:25.657
In frog tadpoles, thyroid hormone drives metamorphosis.

32
00:02:26.897 --> 00:02:32.877
To read any loop, remove or add one hormone, then predict the rest of its loop.
